#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116110} :activity/id #uuid "d07d2d5c-d3e0-595b-84ff-e18597a3d040" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116491 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n- The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n- The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "5a0ce34c-f39a-5c24-bab3-840ff974402c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3050 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "836cc4ee-5cd5-58a8-8fc6-487e670dc062" :step/math-academy-id 16471 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115065 :question/id #uuid "c0c08150-98f9-5677-a7a8-390cffc0c5b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4511"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-poi